I'm police ashay we're now joined  remotely by the Bishop of Dover and the  former chaplain to the Speaker of the  House of Commons Rose Hudson Wilkins  thank you so much for being on the  program today how are you going to be  spending Easter Sunday this year very  different to normal I imagine very very  different indeed I've already taken part  in terms of listening and worshiping  with the archbishop on radio fourth  service and I will be touching in with  other services that are being  live-streamed in and around the diocese  as well today so staying at home that's  the important thing of course staying at  home today of course is a day where many  people would go to church and many  people would spend time with their  families with their loved ones and so  many of our viewers will be unable to do  that very difficult particularly for  those people who are on their own as  well this Easter Sunday what's your  message to them I think my message is  that Easter is about hope and it is  about joy and we should not forget the  joy of those who have survived the virus  including our prime minister the joy as  they leave hospitals and we see people  clapping and sharing and the faces of  those who are caring for them the hope  as well for us today that they the many  acts of kindness that we have seen that  those will continue beyond the corona  virus pandemic and in particular the way  in which we have seen perhaps for the  first time recognized that that our care  workers are cleaners our doctors our  nurses that they are right there on the  frontline and that they are key to the  life of the whole community  those are positive messages and I think  a final message that I would want to  share with everyone is to say please  let's remember that the tomb is empty  Jesus Christ is risen and the  resurrected Christ  comes to us in our homes he comes to us  on the hospital wards he comes to us in  the care rooms  he comes to us right where we are and he  ministers to us so please I know we are  missing our buildings but let's not get  a sort of edifice complex let's just  recognize that the risen Christ comes to  us where we are right there and so let  us acknowledge Christ in our homes let  us stay home protect the NHS and save  lives it's lovely to hear a bit of  positivity at a time that can seem very  bleak I wonder if for the church going  forward there may be new ways of doing  things you've seen more streaming for  example a ways of reaching out to people  in their homes do you think that the  church will change going forward I  sincerely hope that the church will  change you know for such a long time we  have gone into the building and we have  done our religious bit and then we go  away again and we don't do it until we  go back now we're recognizing that  actually that's just a building the  church is the people of God the body of  Christ and so I sincerely hope that one  of the changes we will see is people  actually living their faith in a real  practical upfront way and not hidden in  a building and I hope that as a church  those of us who are leaders within the  church both lay and ordained that we  will use all the various means possible  to equip our people to reach the people  of this land I am just so excited about  all the possibilities that lies ahead  talking about change and you are the  first female black bishop you're  ordained as a priest in 1994 just a  month after it was possible for women to  do so it feels that with every  modernization wave in the church you're  right on  crest of that wave do you feel that the  church still has further to go when it  comes to modernization oh I am sure I am  sure but you know it's it's it's very  difficult to change because very often  we don't look to the spirit we we look  to tradition and we we try to you know  what it is I think those in leadership  within the church and not just those in  leadership  you will hear ordinary congregation  members do they will say but this is the  way we've always done it but you know  what a joy when we are open to what the  Spirit is saying what God is saying to  us through the spirit it brings change  it sweeps through the land and I'm  excited about the possibilities to come  now when I was researching this  interview with you I read a very got a  call that you wrote in January and you  spoke about some people not accepting  your ministry you said when people  rejected me from doing their funerals  because I was black or because I was a  woman more so because I was black I  would allow myself to feel the pain of  that rejection because that pain is real  and there's no point in pretending it's  not now I have to say interview today  you seem like a immensely positive  individual but that must have been quite  difficult to deal with but but you know  that that's what Good Friday was about  Good Friday was about agony and pain and  we cannot skip that so when we  experience who are dying not only in  this country but throughout the world of  this virus we need to acknowledge pain  but we cannot dwell there we have to get  to Easter Sunday resurrection and so we  acknowledge the pain and we say thank  you God you're there with us in the pain  but you are there with us when we come  through that to the joy of Resurrection  and new life because that's what the  Easter is about it is about new life and  new beginnings thank you so much for  being on the program this morning ray is  hard  welcome there Happy Easter
 

